  "raw": {
    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/h2dN7/Chief-Digital-Infrastructure-Officer,2026-25976.html": {
      "title": "Chief Digital & Infrastructure Officer",
      "description": "Chief Digital & Infrastructure Officer -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Chief Digital & Infrastructure Officer Lead the digital and physical foundations of a university focused on unlocking the power of the land. The opportunity Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki Lincoln University is a specialist university focused on the land-based sectors, with an ambitious vision to be a globally ranked, top-five land-based university, enhancing lives and growing the future. Its campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by a network of university-owned farms and research centres. Alongside its digital ecosystem, this infrastructure underpins all of Lincoln’s teaching and research. As Chief Digital and Infrastructure Officer, you will bring these twin foundations together, ensuring networks, cybersecurity, data, information systems, research and teaching infrastructure and property integrate seamlessly to deliver an exceptional experience for students, staff and partners. Why this role, and why now Digital and physical, together. The portfolio spans Information and Technology Services and Property Services, from cybersecurity and data to laboratories and working farms. A breadth few leadership roles can match. Technology as a strategic enabler. Data, artificial intelligence, process transformation and digital experience are reshaping the tertiary sector. You will champion their strategic use across research, teaching, learning and operations to maximise the value of technology investment. A great place to live and lead. Based at Lincoln’s Te Waihora campus, you will enjoy everything Canterbury has to offer, with mountains, coast and the city of Christchurch all close at hand. What you’ll lead Reporting to the Vice-Chancellor, you will provide strategic leadership, oversight and coordination across the University’s digital and infrastructure portfolio. You will: Contribute to the development, implementation and monitoring of University-wide strategies and policies as a collaborative member of the Senior Leadership Team. Provide strategic oversight of digital services, including information systems, cybersecurity, and data management and governance. Ensure the University’s assets and property are aligned with, and supportive of, its teaching, research, partnership and commercial priorities. Support high-quality research and teaching infrastructure, including laboratories, farms, equipment, collections and digital resources. Drive continuous improvement while embedding sound risk management, governance, compliance and health and safety across the portfolio. Advance the University’s commitments under Te Tiriti o Waitangi. What you’ll bring Significant senior leadership experience in a complex organisation, with a track record of influencing decision-making at executive level. Experience leading diverse operational portfolios, such as technology, property, infrastructure, health and safety or other corporate services. Proven success leading organisational change, major projects and service improvement, translating strategic priorities into operational plans and measurable results. Strong commercial acumen and a sound understanding of risk management, governance, compliance and health and safety. The ability to build trusted relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders, and to lead and develop high-performing teams. A collaborative leadership style and a commitment to Manaakitaka and Lincoln University’s values. How to apply Lincoln University has partnered with Sheffield Search for this important appointment. To apply in strict confidence now, visit: https://www.sheffield.co.nz/Job-Search?Id=752 or email your cover letter and CV (in a PDF format wherever possible) to cvwlg@sheffield.co.nz quoting 9113. Applications close on 2 November at 9.00am. Emails will be electronically acknowledged and further correspondence may be by email. To view the Candidate Briefing document, please visit https://www.sheffield.co.nz/Job-Search/ID/752 For more information please phone Patrick Rooney on +64 21 458 362. Job Details Reference # 2026-25976 Posted on 06 Oct 2026 Closes on 02 Nov 2026 09:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise General Mgmt/Executive Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 8 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/tk2N7/M-ori-Pasifika-Student-Academic-Success-Lead,2026-25878.html": {
      "title": "Māori & Pasifika Student Academic Success Lead",
      "description": "Māori & Pasifika Student Academic Success Lead -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Māori & Pasifika Student Academic Success Lead Lead initiatives that support Māori and Pasifika tauira success, using data, partnerships and culturally grounded approaches to improve academic outcomes. Mō te tūka | The role Join our Te Manutaki team and play a pivotal role in advancing Māori and Pasifika student success across Lincoln University. This leadership position focuses on improving academic achievement, progression, retention and completion outcomes for undergraduate and postgraduate tauira through evidence-informed strategies and strong collaboration with faculties, teaching staff and support services. You will lead the implementation of the Manaaki Tauira learner success programme, provide leadership to a small team of advisors, and champion initiatives that uphold Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments while creating meaningful, lasting impact for Māori and Pasifika learners. Some tasks of the role include: Leading initiatives to improve Māori and Pasifika student achievement, retention and progression Using student success data and insights to identify trends, risks and opportunities Partnering with Deans, Programme Directors and academic staff to enhance teaching and learning outcomes Leading and supporting the Manaaki Tauira learner success programme Strengthening postgraduate success, supervision quality and completion outcomes Providing strategic reporting and advice on Māori and Pasifika student success across the University We welcome applications from candidates who can bring lived experience, cultural knowledge, and a passion for empowering Māori and Pasifika tauira to thrive. Competency in te reo Māori and experience working within kaupapa Māori environments are highly valued. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE). The salary for this position is $100,000 - $120,000 per annum, depending on experience. Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have A postgraduate qualification in education, Māori and Indigenous studies, Pacific studies, social sciences, humanities, data science or a related field Significant experience improving student success outcomes within tertiary or secondary education environments Strong analytical, data interpretation and reporting skills, with the ability to translate insights into action Knowledge of Te Ao Māori and/or Pasifika values, with a commitment to supporting culturally responsive practices A sound understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and experience working in partnership to achieve positive outcomes Proven ability to build relationships, influence stakeholders and lead strategic initiatives across complex organisations Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to Merata.Kawharu@lincoln.ac.nz We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. Apply now – we will review applications as they are received and may close the advertising early. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-25878 Posted on 12 Oct 2026 Closes on 08 Nov 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Academic, Administration, Project Management Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) PD Māori Pasifika Student Academic Success Lead September 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 2 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
