    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/7uuN7/Tutor-in-Mircrobiology,2026-26137.html": {
      "title": "Tutor in Mircrobiology",
      "description": "Tutor in Mircrobiology -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Tutor in Mircrobiology Support microbiology teaching, student learning and research activities through tutorials, laboratory teaching and academic support. Mō te tūka | The role Based in our Department of Wine, Food and Molecular Biosciences, this Tutor position plays an important role in supporting high-quality undergraduate teaching while assisting the Head of Department with teaching activities. You'll help deliver engaging learning experiences for students, contribute to microbiology teaching, and support postgraduate research activities within a collaborative academic environment. Some tasks of the role include: Delivering and coordinating undergraduate microbiology tutorials and laboratory classes. Assisting with the development of teaching materials, assessments and course activities. Marking and providing feedback on laboratory reports, assignments and tests. Supporting postgraduate students with the setup, programming and maintenance of research equipment. Providing additional tutorials, coaching and academic support to promote student success. Contributing to innovative learning, teaching and assessment practices across the discipline. Working within the Faculty of Agriculture and Life Sciences, you'll have the opportunity to contribute to student success, teaching innovation, and the development of future graduates in a specialist land-based university. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This fixed term role is from January 2027 to June 2028, based at our Lincoln Campus and works 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE). Salary range: Tutor: Grade A1, $60,293 - $70,000 Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have A completed PhD in Science or a related discipline, or equivalent relevant experience. A strong record of scholarly research, publications and/or a developing research agenda. Proven ability to build positive relationships with students and colleagues from diverse backgrounds. Demonstrated experience collaborating with others in teaching and learning environments. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. A commitment to using technology and innovative approaches to enhance teaching and student learning. Tertiary teaching experience in a relevant field. Experience facilitating learning with undergraduate and postgraduate students. Relevant industry experience. Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to careers@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. Job Details Reference # 2026-26137 Posted on 27 Oct 2026 Closes on 03 Nov 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Academic Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD Tutor October 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
