    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/xaHM7/Lecturer-Senior-Lecturer-in-Accounting-Audit-Tax-,2026-25617.html": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Accounting (Audit & Tax)",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Accounting (Audit & Tax) -\r\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Accounting (Audit & Tax) Deliver high-quality teaching, research and student supervision in accounting and finance, while contributing to industry engagement and academic excellence. Mō te tūka | The role Join our Department of Financial and Business Systems team within the Faculty of Agribusiness and Commerce and help shape the next generation of accounting and finance professionals. Based primarily at our Te Waihora campus, this role combines teaching, research, student supervision, and service contributions across a range of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, including the Bachelor of Commerce and Master of Professional Accounting (CPA). Some tasks of the role include: Teaching undergraduate and postgraduate courses in accounting and finance. Supervising Honours, Master's and PhD research students. Developing and progressing a high-quality research programme and publication record. Designing and updating course content to ensure it remains current and industry relevant. Contributing to learning, teaching and assessment innovation using new technologies. Building relationships with industry, professional bodies and the wider community. This is an exciting opportunity for an emerging academic who is passionate about teaching, building a strong research profile, and contributing to the advancement of accounting and finance education. You'll work collaboratively with colleagues, engage with industry and professional bodies, and support a diverse student community in achieving their goals. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) and commences Semester 1, 2027. Salary range: Lecturer: Grade A6, $88,217 - $100,740 Senior Lecturer: Grade A7, $108,229 – $134,336 Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have: A completed PhD in Accounting. Evidence of successful tertiary-level teaching in accounting, finance, or a related discipline. A developing research profile, including scholarly publications and a clear research agenda. Excellent written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills. The ability to build positive relationships with students, colleagues and external stakeholders from diverse backgrounds. Experience, or a willingness to develop expertise, in supervising student research at Honours and/or Master's level. A commitment to innovation and the use of technology to enhance teaching and research. A relevant professional qualification (e.g. CA, CPA, or equivalent) and knowledge of New Zealand's agribusiness environment would be advantageous. Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to Christopher.Gan@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-25617 Posted on 27 Oct 2026 Closes on 29 Nov 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Academic Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) PD L-SL Accounting September 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and