    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/0BqN7/Lecturer-in-Ecology-Entomology-Plant-Protection,2026-26096.html": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Ecology, Entomology & Plant Protection",
      "description": "Lecturer in Ecology, Entomology & Plant Protection -\r\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Lecturer in Ecology, Entomology & Plant Protection Deliver engaging tertiary teaching in plant pathology while supporting student success, supervision, and academic programme delivery. Mō te tūka | The role Join us as a Lecturer in Plant Pathology and play a key role in delivering high-quality teaching and supporting student learning within our Department of Pest Management and Conservation. This fixed-term, part-time opportunity has been created to provide teaching and administrative support while the Head of Department undertakes a leadership appointment. Working within the Faculty of Agriculture and Life Sciences, you will contribute to a range of undergraduate and postgraduate courses, support student supervision, and help foster an inclusive and engaging learning environment. Based primarily at our Lincoln campus, this role may also involve teaching at other locations in New Zealand and overseas. Some tasks of the role include: Delivering high-quality teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate plant pathology and ecology courses Coordinating courses, assessments, and teaching materials in line with university requirements Supporting and supervising Honours, Masters and PhD students Enhancing student learning through innovative teaching practices and technologies Contributing to curriculum development and continuous course improvement Building positive relationships with colleagues, industry partners, and the wider academic community Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This fixed term role is from January 2027 to December 2028, based at our Lincoln Campus and works 15 hours per week (0.4 FTE). The salary for this position is Grade A6, Step 4: $98,052 per annum (pro rata to part-time hours). Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have A completed PhD in Entomology/Ecology or a closely related discipline, or equivalent qualifications relevant to the role Tertiary-level teaching experience in a relevant field such as entomology, ecology, or related disciplines Strong oral and written communication skills The ability to build positive relationships with students, colleagues, and stakeholders from diverse backgrounds A commitment to providing an inclusive and supportive learning environment An interest in using technology and innovative approaches to enhance teaching and student engagement Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to careers@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. Job Details Reference # 2026-26096 Posted on 27 Oct 2026 Closes on 03 Nov 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Academic Work type(s) Fixed term part-time More details (document) PD Lecturer October 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
