    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/hloN7/Lecturer-Senior-Lecturer-in-Property-Planning-Law,2026-26078.html": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Property, Planning & Law",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Property, Planning & Law -\r\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Property, Planning & Law Teach and research in property, planning and law while shaping future property professionals and supervising postgraduate research. Mō te tūka | The role Based in our Department of Land Management and Systems, this is an exciting opportunity to contribute to teaching, research and academic leadership within the Faculty of Agribusiness and Commerce. You'll play a key role in delivering and developing courses for the Bachelor of Land and Property Management and Graduate Diploma in Property Management and Valuation, while building your research profile and supporting postgraduate students. Some tasks of the role include: Teaching and coordinating courses in property, planning and property law Developing and leading a strong programme of research Publishing in high-quality academic journals and securing research funding Supervising Honours, Masters and PhD students Contributing to curriculum development and innovative teaching practices Building relationships with industry, professional bodies and external stakeholders You will collaborate with colleagues across the University and engage with industry, professional bodies and the wider community to advance the property discipline. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE), commencing 18 th January 2027 The salary range for the role is Lecturer: Grade A6, $88,217 - $100,740 Senior Lecturer: Grade A7, $108,229 – $120,862 Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have A PhD (or close to completion of a PhD) in Property (Real Estate), Planning, or a related discipline A strong track record of research publication and an established research agenda Demonstrated success teaching at tertiary level in a relevant discipline Experience supervising student research at Honours, Masters and/or PhD level Excellent written and verbal communication skills with the ability to engage diverse audiences A collaborative approach, with experience building relationships across academic, professional and industry communities Experience using technology and innovative approaches to enhance teaching and research Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to graham.squires@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-26078 Posted on 27 Oct 2026 Closes on 23 Nov 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Academic Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) PD L-SL Property, Planning Law October 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/ojsN7/International-Student-Advisor,2026-26117.html": {
      "title": "International Student Advisor",
      "description": "International Student Advisor -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek International Student Advisor Provide pastoral care, advice, engagement initiatives and wellbeing support to help international students thrive at university. Mō te tūka | The role The International Student Advisor plays a key role in ensuring international students have a positive, supportive and successful experience at Lincoln University. You'll provide pastoral care, guidance and advocacy to students throughout their journey, helping them navigate life and study in Aotearoa while supporting the University's commitments under the Education (Pastoral Care of Tertiary and International Learners) Code of Practice 2021. Some tasks of the role include: Providing pastoral care, advice and support to international students, including emergency support when required. Supporting students as they transition to life and study in New Zealand. Developing and delivering orientation, pre-departure and engagement initiatives. Monitoring student wellbeing, engagement and academic progress, implementing early intervention support where needed. Connecting students with appropriate University and community support services. Developing resources and communications that enhance the international student experience. Working closely with students, families, academic staff and support services, you'll deliver proactive wellbeing and engagement initiatives, monitor student success, and help create a welcoming and inclusive environment where students feel connected and empowered to achieve their goals. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 22.5 hours per week (0.6 FTE). The salary for this position is $75,000 – $82,000 per annum (pro rata to part-time hours), depending on experience. Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have A relevant tertiary qualification and/or significant equivalent professional experience. Experience working in a similar advisory, wellbeing or student support role within a complex organisation. Demonstrated experience supporting people from diverse cultural, ethnic and social backgrounds. Knowledge of, and experience working within, the Education (Pastoral Care of Tertiary and International Learners) Code of Practice 2021. Excellent relationship-building, communication and stakeholder engagement skills. Experience using CRM, student management or similar systems, with strong organisational skills and sound judgement in managing complex situations. A current driver's licence. Ability to participate in an on-call arrangement to respond to emergency international student pastoral care needs. Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to Lucy.Page-Dalton@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. Apply now – we will review applications as they are received and may close the advertising early. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-26117 Posted on 29 Oct 2026 Closes on 20 Nov 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Administration, Customer Service, Health Work type(s) Permanent part-time More details (document) PD International Student Advisor October 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 10 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
